<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Tyre</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 BCE – 332 BCE (approx.) · capital Tyre</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tyre, the island city that led Phoenician trade across the Mediterranean, founded Carthage and resisted Alexander in a seven-month siege.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tyre was built on an offshore island with a mainland suburb, giving it a nearly impregnable harbour. It was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> age under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abimilki">Abimilki</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a> it emerged in the 10th century BCE as the strongest Phoenician city.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiram-i">Hiram I</a>, ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel</a>, ruled a merchant state with the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melqart">Melqart</a> at its heart. Tyre produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple">murex</a> purple dye and glass, and its sailors founded colonies across the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, traditionally in 814 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tyre paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> and survived a 13-year siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> it kept its kings. In 332 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> built a causeway, stormed the island and sold its people into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tyre.jpg" alt="Tyre" width="155" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Historical Atlas by William R. Shepherd 1911 edition · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plan_of_Tyre_333-332_B.C._(William_R._Shepherd).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 BCE – 332 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tyre</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hiram I, Ithobaal I, Pygmalion</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Phoenician religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Phoenician</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Lebanon</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Tyre, Sur</dd></dl></aside></div>
